    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Cats experience a room very differently from us. They walk along the back of the sofa, investigate every shelf, chew anything leafy and groom whatever settles on their fur. A beautifully styled room can hide several hazards for a curious cat, from a vase of lilies to a reed diffuser on a low side table.</p>
<p>The good news is that a cat-friendly home does not have to look like a pet shop. With a few well-informed choices about plants, scents and how you display things, you can keep a calm, layered, grown-up look. This guide covers what to avoid, what tends to be safer and how to style with a cat in mind. It is a design guide, not veterinary advice: for anything specific to your cat, ask your vet.</p>

<h2 id="start-with-the-plant-lists">Start with the plant lists, not the plant shop</h2>
<p>Before you buy any plant or cut flowers for a home with cats, check it against a reliable reference. The ASPCA (the American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals) publishes searchable toxic and non-toxic plant lists for cats, and these are the lists most designers and vets point people to. Search by the plant’s botanical (Latin) name as well as its common name, because common names vary and several unrelated plants can share one.</p>
<p>Make it a habit to check gifts too. Bouquets often arrive with lilies tucked in, and seasonal plants come and go through the year.</p>

<h2 id="lilies">Lilies: the one to remember</h2>
<p>True lilies (<em>Lilium</em> species, such as Easter, tiger, Asiatic and oriental lilies) and daylilies (<em>Hemerocallis</em>) are extremely dangerous to cats. Every part of the plant is considered toxic, including the leaves, petals, pollen and even the water in the vase. A cat may only need to brush past the flowers and lick pollen off its fur to be at risk, and lily poisoning can cause serious kidney damage.</p>
<ul>
<li>Do not bring true lilies or daylilies into a home with cats at all, even in a room you think the cat cannot reach.</li>
<li>Ask friends and florists to leave lilies out of bouquets sent to you.</li>
<li>If you think your cat has had any contact with a lily, contact your vet or an emergency vet immediately rather than waiting for symptoms.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="other-plants-to-avoid">Other common houseplants to be careful with</h2>
<p>Many popular decorative plants appear on the ASPCA’s toxic list for cats. Effects range from mouth irritation and stomach upset to much more serious harm, depending on the plant and the amount. Common examples include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pothos (devil’s ivy)</strong> and <strong>dieffenbachia (dumb cane)</strong>: contain irritating calcium oxalate crystals.</li>
<li><strong>Sago palm</strong>: highly toxic, and particularly dangerous; keep it out of homes with pets.</li>
<li><strong>Monstera, philodendron, peace lily and snake plant</strong>: all widely listed as toxic to cats (despite its name, the peace lily is not a true lily, but it is still an irritant).</li>
<li><strong>Aloe vera, jade plant and many euphorbias</strong>: also commonly listed as toxic.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is not a complete list. If a plant is not clearly listed as non-toxic, treat it as a risk until you have checked.</p>

<h2 id="safer-plant-choices">Plants commonly listed as non-toxic</h2>
<p>Several attractive houseplants are listed as non-toxic to cats on the ASPCA list. Good options for a styled interior include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spider plant</strong> (<em>Chlorophytum comosum</em>): easy, trailing and good on a high shelf.</li>
<li><strong>Boston fern</strong> (<em>Nephrolepis exaltata</em>): soft and full; lovely in a bathroom or on a plant stand.</li>
<li><strong>Parlour palm</strong> (<em>Chamaedorea elegans</em>): a graceful floor or table palm for a lower-light corner. Make sure it is not confused with the sago palm, which is not a true palm.</li>
<li><strong>Calathea</strong> and other prayer plants: patterned leaves that add colour without flowers.</li>
<li><strong>Peperomia</strong> and <strong>African violet</strong>: compact choices for side tables.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two cautions. First, always check the exact species, because one genus can contain both safe and unsafe plants. Second, “non-toxic” does not mean “meant to be eaten”: a cat that chews a lot of any plant may still be sick. Pot plants in heavy, wide-based planters, cover the soil with large pebbles if your cat likes to dig, and check that any fertiliser, leaf shine or pest treatment is suitable for homes with pets.</p>

<h2 id="scents-diffusers-and-candles">Scents, diffusers and candles</h2>
<p>Home fragrance is where many otherwise careful owners get caught out. Cats are known to be particularly sensitive to many essential oils, and concentrated oils, whether licked, absorbed through the skin or groomed off the fur, can be harmful.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Essential oil diffusers:</strong> many vets advise against using them around cats, especially in small or poorly ventilated rooms and with oils such as tea tree, eucalyptus, citrus, peppermint and pine. Ask your vet before using one.</li>
<li><strong>Reed diffusers:</strong> the open bottle is easy to knock over, and spilt oil on paws or fur is then groomed off. If you keep one, place it somewhere the cat genuinely cannot reach.</li>
<li><strong>Candles and wax melts:</strong> the obvious risk is an open flame near a tail or whiskers, and a curious cat can knock a candle over. Never leave a lit candle unattended, and consider flameless LED candles for low surfaces.</li>
<li><strong>Liquid potpourri and room sprays:</strong> keep these well out of reach and let surfaces dry before the cat walks on them.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your cat shows signs such as drooling, coughing, laboured breathing, wobbliness or vomiting after exposure to a scented product, move them to fresh air and call your vet.</p>

<h2 id="breakables-and-display">Breakables and how to display them</h2>
<!--figure:styled-cosy-living-room|Ceramic vases and dried grasses styled on low surfaces: in a home with cats, heavier, wide-based pieces are the more practical choice.-->
<p>Cats like to sit on exactly the surfaces we style. Rather than abandoning accessories, style with weight and stability in mind.</p>
<ul>
<li>Choose heavy, wide-based ceramics and stone pieces over tall, slim glass vases.</li>
<li>Keep the most precious or fragile items behind glass, in a cabinet, or on a shelf with no landing spot nearby.</li>
<li>Use museum putty or small removable adhesive dots to stop lighter objects from sliding off shelves (test on a hidden spot first, especially on painted or antique finishes).</li>
<li>Leave a clear runway on at least one window sill or shelf so your cat has a place to sit that is not a styling display.</li>
<li>Avoid small, swallowable decorative items such as loose beads, string, ribbon, tinsel and elastic bands, which can cause serious problems if eaten.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="cords-and-blind-cords">Cords, cables and blind cords</h2>
<p>Dangling cords look like toys. Chewed cables are a shock and fire risk, and looped blind or curtain cords can be a strangulation hazard for pets as well as young children.</p>
<ul>
<li>Run lamp and charger cables behind furniture or through cable covers and trunking.</li>
<li>Fit cord cleats or tensioners so blind cords are held taut and high, or choose cordless blinds and wand-operated curtains.</li>
<li>Unplug and put away chargers when not in use, especially if your cat is a chewer.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="stable-furniture">Stable furniture and climbing routes</h2>
<p>Cats climb, so assume tall furniture will be climbed. Secure bookcases, tall cabinets and freestanding shelving to the wall with anti-tip fixings, following the manufacturer’s instructions. If you rent, check your tenancy agreement and ask your landlord for written permission before drilling. Avoid perching lamps, plants or vases on top of tall units where a leaping cat could send them flying.</p>
<p>Better still, design a route. A run of floating shelves, a sturdy cat tree beside a window, or a wide sill cushion gives your cat a sanctioned place to be up high, which makes them less likely to explore your styled shelves.</p>

<h2 id="scratching-zones">Scratching zones that work with your scheme</h2>
<p>Scratching is natural behaviour, so give it somewhere to go. Place a sturdy scratching post near the sofa or the spot your cat already favours, because cats often scratch where they like to stretch on waking. A post should be heavy enough not to wobble and tall enough for your cat to stretch fully, often around 80 cm (31 in) or more for an adult cat.</p>
<ul>
<li>Choose posts and pads in sisal, jute or natural timber that sit comfortably alongside a neutral scheme.</li>
<li>Add a flat scratch pad by the front door or beside a rug if your cat scratches horizontally.</li>
<li>Choose flat-weave or tight cut-pile rugs over loop-pile, which claws can snag and pull.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Make a single “cat-safe” checklist on your phone for plant shopping and gifts: check the botanical name on the ASPCA list, check the pot is heavy enough, and check where it will sit before it comes home.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="quick-checklist">A quick cat-friendly styling checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>No true lilies or daylilies, ever, including in bouquets.</li>
<li>Every plant checked against the ASPCA list by botanical name.</li>
<li>Diffusers and oils discussed with your vet and kept out of reach.</li>
<li>Flameless candles on any surface a cat can reach.</li>
<li>Heavy, stable accessories; fragile pieces behind glass.</li>
<li>Cables hidden, blind cords secured or removed.</li>
<li>Tall furniture anchored to the wall.</li>
<li>A scratching post and a high perch in the main living space.</li>
</ul>

